WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:05.510
I'm going to demonstrate how to use CytoCV to automate measurement of nuclear to cytoplasmic

2
00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:09.150
intensity ratios for a fluorescently tagged protein.

3
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:14.070
This experiment requires a fluorescent protein that will define the location of the nucleus.

4
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:18.430
Here I'm using a nucleoporin protein that is tagged with a red fluorophore.

5
00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:23.970
This red image shows the cell contour, which was obtained from the DIC image, and the outline

6
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.710
of the red nucleus.

7
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.190
The green images show my protein of interest, which is tagged with a green fluorophore and

8
00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.150
accumulates in the nucleus in two distinct puncta.

9
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:37.830
Visually, we can see that this mutant on the bottom does not enter the nucleus as efficiently

10
00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.910
as the wild type on top.

11
00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:44.590
I'm now going to use CytoCV to calculate the difference in the nuclear versus cytoplasmic

12
00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:51.240
intensity ratio of this green protein in individual cells for these two strains.

13
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.400
I have already signed in and uploaded two files, one each for wild type and mutant.

14
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.480
I will use the statistics plugin options to select the type of analysis that I want

15
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.240
to perform with these images.

16
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.920
First, I need to decide what types of cells to include in the analysis.

17
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.260
The cells for this experiment have been arrested in metaphase of mitosis and should therefore

18
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.670
be large-budded cell pairs, so I'll select the cell pairs only option.

19
00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:21.270
Under signal quantification, I will choose nuclear cell pair intensity.

20
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.870
Under nucleus contour source, I need to choose the appropriate color option for this

21
00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:26.870
experiment.

22
00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:32.030
By selecting the red nucleus, measure green option, because I want to tell CytoCV to use

23
00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:36.410
the red protein to make the nuclear contour, and then measure the intensity of the green

24
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:39.020
protein within that contour.

25
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.780
For the nucleus contour mode, I'm choosing the balanced option.

26
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.140
The signal for my nuclear protein can often be speckled, and I want all of those speckles

27
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.860
combined together into a single contour.

28
00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:54.220
In the aggressive mode, CytoCV will split up the speckles into different contours.

29
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.860
I'll now turn off anything else selected that is irrelevant for what I want to analyze.

30
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.780
CytoCV can automatically identify and assign channel roles when sufficient metadata is

31
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.340
available.

32
00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.980
Because my older source files do not contain the required metadata, I'm going to manually

33
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:16.860
set the channels by defining the order of the fluorescent images in my TIFF stack here.

34
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.580
Now I'll close this window and pre-process the files,

35
00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.370
and then start the analysis.

36
00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:37.470
I've now switched to a window in which the analysis has finished, and I can see the pairs

37
00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.710
of cells that CytoCV has identified to analyze.

38
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:45.870
Below, I can visualize each individual cell pair and determine whether I want to keep that

39
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.870
cell for the analysis.

40
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:51.990
For this experiment, I want to analyze only cells in metaphase with a single nucleus

41
00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.950
between the two cells.

42
00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:58.430
In this first cell, there is a single red nuclear contour that spans the bud neck,

43
00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.270
so this is the stage of cell that I want to quantitate.

44
00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:06.310
I'm going to click through each cell and make sure that I want to keep it for the analysis.

45
00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.900
All of these cells so far look good.

46
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.380
But here in cell 9, we have a problem.

47
00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.700
The red contour doesn't match visually what I see as the red nuclear signal.

48
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:22.060
If I were to include this cell, the nucleus would be defined as larger than it actually is.

49
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.180
This cell is also problematic because there are two distinct red contours,

50
00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.260
meaning the cell has already undergone anaphase.

51
00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:33.420
To remove this cell from the analysis, I will click to the right of the cell and select

52
00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.420
the delete cell option.

53
00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:40.380
I'm going to delete cell 10 as well because it also has gone through anaphase.

54
00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.540
Cell 11 looks fine.

55
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.660
And now I'm back to cell 1.

56
00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.450
Now I'll go on to the mutant image.

57
00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:52.070
Looking through these cells, all of them fit my criteria.

58
00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:55.170
At this point, I'm ready to download the numerical data.

59
00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:59.310
This table at the bottom has columns for all the types of analysis that CytoCV can

60
00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.790
do, most of which we're ignoring for this experiment.

61
00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:06.910
The numbers that are relevant to us are the ones at the very end of the table.

62
00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:10.070
To export the data in a format that's easier for me to use,

63
00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.390
I'll click Download Statistics.

64
00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:16.590
I would like to view the data in Excel, so I'll select that option.

65
00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:19.430
Most of these measurements are irrelevant to the current experiment,

66
00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.670
and the ones at the bottom that pertain to my analysis are already checked.

67
00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.430
To ensure that I will be downloading data for all the files

68
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.470
that I have analyzed in my session, I will click Edit Files, Select All.

69
00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.180
And now I'm ready to download.

70
00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:38.940
When I open this Excel file, I see the name of each image file on the left,

71
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.180
followed by all of the measurements and calculations

72
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.220
that I had previously selected.

73
00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:46.620
Notice that cells 9 and 10 in the wild type image are missing.

74
00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.820
This is because I deleted them.

75
00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:53.160
By uploading many files with images similar to these and downloading the data,

76
00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.220
I was able to generate a graph in GraphPad Prism

77
00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:01.140
that displays the ratios for over 100 cells of both the wild type and the mutant.

78
00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.140
The calculations show that the mutant protein of interest

79
00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:08.100
has a lower nuclear to cytoplasmic intensity ratio than the wild type,

80
00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.180
which was expected based on what I can see visually in these images.

81
00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.700
The difference is highly significant.

82
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.740
So that's how CytoCV can be used

83
00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.260
to calculate nuclear versus cytoplasmic intensities.

84
00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:24.100
For other types of analysis, I would follow a similar process,

85
00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.380
but choose different options in the statistics plug-in section.
